[method]
The paper describes likelihood-based cross-validation as a statistical method for evaluating probabilistic neural models after maximum-likelihood fitting. In this approach, competing models pA(y|x, θA) and pB(y|x, θB) are first fit to training data, and then their generalization performance is compared by evaluating the likelihood of independent test data, pA(y*|x*, θA) versus pB(y*|x*, θB). The test data can be obtained by presenting new stimuli x* to the neuron and recording new spike responses y*, or by holding out a portion of the original data. The method is motivated by the need to avoid overfitting: a model with more parameters may assign higher likelihood to the training data but may perform poorly on new data. In the context of the proposed research plan, this method could be used after collecting responses from approximately 100 MT neurons to compare candidate encoding models for bi-speed stimuli, such as averaging, soft-max, slow-speed bias, fast-speed bias, or the proposed modified divisive normalization model. It is not a data-collection technique, but it is relevant to the computational modeling and model-validation components of the overall plan.
[results]
The provided sections do not report a quantitative experimental outcome from applying likelihood-based cross-validation to a specific neural dataset. Instead, they provide a conceptual toy example illustrating overfitting. For five data points drawn from a Gaussian distribution, model A is a single Gaussian fitted with the sample mean and standard deviation, while model B is a mixture of five very narrow Gaussians centered on the five sample points. Model B assigns higher likelihood to the training points because it concentrates probability mass near them, but it is expected to assign very low probability to new data points that do not lie near the original samples. No numerical likelihood values, statistical significance tests, or empirical performance comparisons are reported in the provided text.
[context]
The method is defined for probabilistic spike-response models p(y|x, θ) fitted by maximum likelihood. It requires a training set for parameter estimation and a separate test set of stimuli and spike responses, either newly generated or held out from the original data. The excerpt does not specify dataset size, species, recording hardware, stimulus parameters, number of parameters, regularization strength, or other implementation details. It is not tied to area MT, macaque monkeys, fixation tasks, direction tuning, receptive-field mapping, speed tuning, or bi-speed stimuli. Its relevance to the proposed method is indirect: it provides a validation framework for models built from the planned MT recordings, rather than a method for collecting the neural data themselves.
